WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.790 --> 00:00:05.500
The hockey mask-wearing, machete-toting Jason
Voorhees has terrorized unsuspecting teens

00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:08.370
since Friday the 13th hit screens in 1980.

00:00:08.370 --> 00:00:12.929
The franchise has produced 12 movies, a TV
show, a series of comics, and a few brutal

00:00:12.929 --> 00:00:15.589
video games…but audiences still want more.

00:00:15.589 --> 00:00:18.980
Take a look behind the mask to see how he
became one of the most recognizable movie

00:00:18.980 --> 00:00:20.620
monsters of all time.

00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:24.080
Here's the untold truth of Friday the 13th.

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:25.230
Save the date

00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:29.500
Following his work on Wes Craven's The Last
House on the Left, director/producer Sean

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:33.100
Cunningham sought to capitalize on the growing
slasher movie trend.

00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:37.220
In a pretty brilliant marketing move, Cunningham
took out an ad promoting the film before he

00:00:37.220 --> 00:00:38.750
had any financial backing.

00:00:38.750 --> 00:00:43.830
He called the unscripted film Friday the 13th,
mimicking the date-specific theme set by the

00:00:43.830 --> 00:00:45.080
Halloween series.

00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:49.140
The strategy worked, and as word spread of
what the ads called "the most terrifying movie

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:52.080
ever made," investors actually started reaching
out to him.

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:56.230
He found himself with a budget for a film
with a title — but no plot or characters.

00:00:56.230 --> 00:00:58.140
So why Friday the 13th?

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:02.810
"Jason was my son, and today is his birthday."

00:01:02.810 --> 00:01:04.500
Joshua Voorhees?

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:08.970
Miller originally wrote the character of Jason
Voorhees as "Josh," but changed it to avoid

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:11.650
giving him the same name as his own son, Joshua.

00:01:11.650 --> 00:01:15.430
And because, really, Josh Takes Manhattan
probably wouldn't have quite the same ring

00:01:15.430 --> 00:01:16.430
to it.

00:01:16.430 --> 00:01:19.930
At the time, the character's name had little
significance—he wasn't even written to appear

00:01:19.930 --> 00:01:25.260
in the film until later drafts, and even then,
only in his mother's dream sequences.

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:26.990
Masked Maniac

00:01:26.990 --> 00:01:31.090
Jason didn't take his iconic, masked form
until Friday the 13th: Part III.

00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:35.340
While the original character was created by
screenwriter Victor Miller and director Sean

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:40.370
S. Cunningham, the hockey mask was a contribution
from Part III director Steve Miner to replace

00:01:40.370 --> 00:01:42.310
Jason's usual burlap sack.

00:01:42.310 --> 00:01:50.930
"Steve had done a whole bunch of hockey documentaries
when he was learning the editing craft.

00:01:50.930 --> 00:01:55.760
And I think that just out of that he said
'well let's at least try this.'

00:01:55.760 --> 00:01:59.939
And that was the choice for Friday 3 and it
just stuck."

00:01:59.939 --> 00:02:05.091
The mask worn by Jason in Part III was replicated
from a Detroit Red Wings goalie mask, and

00:02:05.091 --> 00:02:07.909
variations appeared throughout the rest of
the series.

00:02:07.909 --> 00:02:12.099
Miner's suggestion of the hockey mask secured
Jason as an iconic figure, making him the

00:02:12.099 --> 00:02:14.909
master of terror that we know today.

00:02:14.909 --> 00:02:16.450
Camp Crystal Lake

00:02:16.450 --> 00:02:21.250
Camp No-Be-Bo-Sco, or the North Bergen Boy
Scouts camp, and has been operational since

00:02:21.250 --> 00:02:24.739
1927, but you might know it better as Camp
Crystal Lake.

00:02:24.739 --> 00:02:29.220
The New Jersey summer camp runs regular day
tours of the grounds to satisfy the curiosities

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:34.650
of horror fans, but No-Be-Bo-Sco is otherwise
reserved exclusively for Boy Scout activities.

00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:38.269
According to the official site, the hundred
dollar tickets sell out quickly, but they're

00:02:38.269 --> 00:02:41.489
good for a five-hour guided tour of all the
key massacre locations.

00:02:41.489 --> 00:02:45.829
If you can't make it out to Jason's bloodstained
stomping grounds, you can buy a chunk of the

00:02:45.829 --> 00:02:50.719
original Camp Crystal Lake dock, a slice of
log cabin, or a bottle of water from the lake

00:02:50.719 --> 00:02:53.470
in which young Jason famously drowned.

00:02:53.470 --> 00:02:54.470
Mommy

00:02:57.140 --> 00:03:02.000
Friday the 13th's signature sound cue is actually
the voice of composer Harry Manfredini.

00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:05.840
Cunningham approached Manfredini to create
a musical cue that would characterize the

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:07.609
presence of the unseen psycho.

00:03:07.609 --> 00:03:12.189
Manfredini took a cue from the film itself,
when Jason's mother utters those terrifying

00:03:12.189 --> 00:03:13.189
words...

00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:19.030
Manfredini repeated the words, applied a heavy
reverb to his voice, and the result became

00:03:19.030 --> 00:03:19.750
iconic.

00:03:22.389 --> 00:03:27.090
The effect that was initially created to foreshadow
Mrs. Voorhees would cue the presence of Jason

00:03:27.090 --> 00:03:28.450
in all the new films to follow.

00:03:31.159 --> 00:03:32.680
A new car

00:03:32.680 --> 00:03:36.569
After failing to cast Shelley Winters for
the role of Mrs. Voorhees, the production

00:03:36.569 --> 00:03:41.090
team sought out veteran television actress
Betsy Palmer, who initially turned it down

00:03:41.090 --> 00:03:44.019
upon reading the script, which she thought
was awful.

00:03:44.019 --> 00:03:48.059
Palmer eventually caved and took the role
because she desperately needed a new car.

00:03:48.059 --> 00:03:51.870
"And truly I only did the darn thing to buy
a car, I really did."

00:03:51.870 --> 00:03:54.549
At the end of filming, she could afford a
Volkswagen Scirocco.

00:03:54.549 --> 00:03:58.790
She changed her mind after production began
when effects artist Tom Savini showed her

00:03:58.790 --> 00:04:00.579
some pictures of his effects work.

00:04:00.579 --> 00:04:04.299
When a picture of the effects for the young,
deformed Jason came up, he told her, "That's

00:04:04.299 --> 00:04:05.299
your son."

00:04:05.299 --> 00:04:09.340
From that point on, Palmer gained sympathy
for Pamela Voorhees and delivered a performance

00:04:09.340 --> 00:04:11.420
that would become a milestone of her career.

00:04:11.420 --> 00:04:13.180
"Hahahaha chechecheche"

00:04:13.180 --> 00:04:14.689
Best body count

00:04:14.689 --> 00:04:19.900
Jason's victims have been tallied at numbers
ranging between 158 and 300, but it's still

00:04:19.900 --> 00:04:24.650
unanimous that Jason is responsible for more
onscreen fatalities than any of his horror

00:04:24.650 --> 00:04:25.920
villain contemporaries.

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:30.160
The wide margin of error is attributed to
Mrs. Voorhees being the original maniac, a

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:35.180
grief-sick father emulating Jason in Part
V, and because much of his body count in Freddy

00:04:35.180 --> 00:04:38.700
Vs. Jason are attributable to Freddy via mind
control.

00:04:38.700 --> 00:04:43.110
Even the lower-end figure lands Jason miles
ahead of Freddy Krueger, Michael Myers, or

00:04:43.110 --> 00:04:44.110
Leatherface.

00:04:44.110 --> 00:04:45.110
Thanks for watching!

00:04:45.110 --> 00:04:48.340
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw.

00:04:48.340 --> 00:04:51.500
And leave us a comment to let us know which
Friday the 13th film is your favorite...

